    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nA local `dict[...]` annotation is enforced as an exact `dict` at runtime even when `annotation_typing=False`. Assigning a `collections.defaultdict` works in ordinary Python, but the compiled module raises `TypeError` when a comprehension accesses the value. I reproduced it with the global compiler directive, the file-level directive, and both directives together.\n\nPython and mypy accept defaultdict -> dict casting because defaultdict subclasses dict. Cython appears to have an optimized dict implementation and when the code reaches .items(), it checks for an exact dict, raising the exception.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n`repro.py`:\n\n```python\n# cython: annotation_typing=False\n\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\n\ndef reproduce():\n    values: dict[str, dict[str, int]] = defaultdict(lambda: defaultdict(int))\n    values[\"target\"][\"severity\"] += 1\n    return [counts for _, counts in values.items()]\n```\n\n`setup.py`:\n\n```python\nfrom Cython.Build import cythonize\nfrom setuptools import Extension, setup\n\n\nsetup(\n    ext_modules=cythonize(\n        [Extension(\"repro\", [\"repro.py\"])],\n        compiler_directives={\"annotation_typing\": False, \"language_level\": \"3\"},\n    )\n)\n```\n\nBuild and run:\n\n```console\n$ python setup.py build_ext --inplace\n$ python -c 'import repro; print(repro.reproduce())'\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<string>\", line 1, in <module>\n  File \"repro.py\", line 9, in repro.reproduce\n    return [counts for _, counts in values.items()]\nTypeError: Expected dict, got collections.defaultdict\n```\n\nRunning the source as ordinary Python succeeds:\n\n```console\n$ python -c 'import runpy; namespace = runpy.run_path(\"repro.py\"); print(namespace[\"reproduce\"]())'\n[defaultdict(<class 'int'>, {'severity': 1})]\n```\n\nThe issue is still present on master as of `dbbbdbb97f116c46495b5ab88ba0195a277016f9`\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nWith `annotation_typing=False`, Cython should ignore the local `dict[...]` annotation and preserve Python's acceptance of the `defaultdict` subclass. The compiled function should return the same value as ordinary Python.\n\n### OS\n\nUbuntu 25.10\n\n### Python version\n\nPython 3.14.3\n\n### Cython version\n\nCython 3.3.0\n\n### Additional context\n\nCython documents exact built-in types, including rejecting subclasses of an annotated `dict`, when annotation typing is enabled. The unexpected part is that the exact-type behavior remains when annotation typing is explicitly disabled.\n\nThis is superficially similar to #5246, but that issue involved Cython 0.29.33 with `distutils` and allowed the file-level directive as a workaround, whereas this reproduces with Cython 3.3.0 using `setuptools`/`cythonize` and fails with either the file-level or global directive alone.",
